					<item>
				<guid>http://www.scpwiki.com/forum/t-235749#post-2321338</guid>
				<title>(no title)</title>
				<link>http://www.scpwiki.com/forum/t-235749/scp-585#post-2321338</link>
				<description></description>
				<pubDate>Sun, 21 Jun 2015 04:51:55 +0000</pubDate>
				<wikidot:authorName>Doctor GE Smith</wikidot:authorName>				<wikidot:authorUserId>1786409</wikidot:authorUserId>				<content:encoded>
					<![CDATA[
						 <p>I saw this a while ago, and now +1ed.<br /> For the people concerned with the structural integrity of the graphite: this is clearly not actual sharpness, but conceptual sharpness. Technically, it would not be possible to make a material blade that could slice atoms (unless you use something like neutronium), because the sharpest you could get would still be 1 atom across. This device is clearly imbuing the concept of sharpness, and therefore the fragility of the point would remain that of normal sharp pencil lead, and it would penetrate things the way that seems intuitively right- not scientifically right.<br /> Or at least that's my interpretation.</p> 
				 	]]>
				</content:encoded>							</item>
